# AstraOS Project Rules

- `docs/PRD.md` is the single source of truth for scope, requirements, and acceptance
  criteria. Before doing any work, check it — don't guess at scope or invent structure
  it doesn't define.
- Work strictly phase by phase, in the order defined in the PRD's Roadmap section
  (Phase 0 → Phase 14). Never start a new phase until the current one is confirmed
  complete against its Exit/Acceptance Criteria.
- Never mark a phase, driver, or deliverable "done" unless it actually meets the
  acceptance criteria in the PRD. If something is incomplete or broken, say so
  explicitly — don't round up.
- Every lesson doc follows the PRD's Documentation Standard: all 14 sections, including
  a real Troubleshooting & Common Pitfalls section (actual error messages, not
  placeholders), and the Guide Writing Standards (define acronyms on first use,
  copy-paste-runnable commands with expected output).
- Follow the repo structure and commit convention exactly as defined in the PRD
  (`docs:`, `kernel:`, `driver:`, `boot:`, `rootfs:`, `experiment:`, `fix:`, etc.).
- Commit after every meaningful unit of work — one lesson, one driver, one config
  change. Never leave more than one logical change uncommitted. Push after committing.
- At every versioned checkpoint (v0.1, v0.5, v1.0, v2.0), follow the PRD's release
  process: flashable image, checksum, flashing instructions — and confirm the image
  has actually been boot-tested before treating it as ready. Never publish an
  untested image.
- If something in the PRD's Risk Register is actually happening, flag it immediately
  instead of quietly working around it.
- If scope is unclear, check the PRD's Non-Goals section before assuming.
- If we deviate from the PRD on purpose, say so — the PRD should be updated to match
  reality, not silently ignored.
